✅ HashMap vs Hashtable in Java

| Feature              | **HashMap**                                              | **Hashtable**                                              |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Thread Safety**    | ❌ Not thread-safe                                        | ✅ Thread-safe (methods synchronized)                       |
| **Performance**      | ✅ Faster (no sync overhead)                              | ❌ Slower (locks whole table per method)                    |
| **Null keys/values** | ✅ **1 null key** + multiple null values                  | ❌ No null key or null value                                |
| **Introduced in**    | Java 1.2 (Collections Framework)                         | Java 1.0 (legacy)                                          |
| **Order**            | ❌ No insertion/sorted order                              | ❌ No insertion/sorted order                                |
| **Iterator**         | **Fail-fast** (`ConcurrentModificationException`)        | **Fail-fast** Iterator; older **Enumeration** is not fail-fast |
| **Extends / parent** | `AbstractMap`                                            | `Dictionary` (legacy) + also implements `Map`              |
| **Use case**         | Default map for most apps                                | Legacy only — prefer `ConcurrentHashMap` today             |

────────────────────────────────────────
🔍 Examples

```java
// HashMap — nulls allowed
Map<String, String> map = new HashMap<>();
map.put(null, "value");   // ✔
map.put("key", null);     // ✔

// Hashtable — nulls forbidden
Map<String, String> table = new Hashtable<>();
table.put(null, "value"); // ❌ NullPointerException
table.put("key", null);   // ❌ NullPointerException
```

────────────────────────────────────────
📌 HashMap + insertion order → `LinkedHashMap`
`HashMap` does **not** keep insertion order.
`LinkedHashMap` (subclass of `HashMap`) keeps insertion order via a doubly linked list.

```java
Map<String, String> ordered = new LinkedHashMap<>();
ordered.put("b", "2");
ordered.put("a", "1");
// iteration order: b, a
```

────────────────────────────────────────
🔐 When to use what

| Scenario                              | Recommendation                                  |
| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Single-threaded / general use         | ✅ `HashMap`                                     |
| Need insertion order                  | ✅ `LinkedHashMap`                               |
| Multithreaded modern app              | ✅ `ConcurrentHashMap` (not Hashtable)           |
| Legacy code only                      | `Hashtable` if you must                         |

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary
- Prefer **`HashMap`** in modern apps.
- Avoid **`Hashtable`** except legacy maintenance.
- For concurrency, use **`ConcurrentHashMap`** — finer locking / CAS, better throughput than Hashtable.

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) Hashtable Iterator is **fail-fast** (like HashMap). Don’t say “Hashtable iterator is fail-safe.” Older `Enumeration` is the non-fail-fast one.
2) Hashtable is synchronized but still weaker than ConcurrentHashMap for high concurrency (whole-table lock).
3) HashMap allows nulls; Hashtable and ConcurrentHashMap do **not**.
4) Neither HashMap nor Hashtable maintains insertion order — use LinkedHashMap for that.
5) Synchronized ≠ always enough for compound actions (`check-then-act` still needs care).
